{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כפר לעמך ישראל", :translit "kaper le'amekha yisra'el", :gloss "Atone for Your people Israel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Derivation of arevut from the communal atonement language of the eglah arufah ritual."}], :frame {:text "The commandment of eglah arufah (Deuteronomy 21:1–9) concludes with the elders declaring 'kaper le'amekha yisra'el' — 'Atone for Your people Israel.' Since the entire community must seek atonement for an unsolved murder they did not personally commit, this verse is read as the scriptural basis for the principle of mutual responsibility (arevut) among the Jewish people. The principle of arevut is addressed in the Talmud; for example, Shavuot 39a states 'kol Yisra'el arevim zeh ba-zeh,' and the extension of korbanot to atone for those who did not sin is discussed at Shavuot 39b.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:8" "Shavuot 39a" "Shavuot 39b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כפר לעמך ישראל אשר פדית", :translit "kaper le'amekha yisra'el asher padita", :gloss "Atone for Your people Israel whom You redeemed"} {:he "זה הכהנים", :translit "zeh ha-kohanim", :gloss "this, the priests"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "כפר לעמך ישראל אשר פדית", :spells "כליא"} {:kind :gematria, :a "כליא", :b "זה הכהנים"}], :frame {:text "The priests serve as agents of atonement for the nation, as stated at Numbers 18:1 regarding the priestly house bearing the iniquity of the sanctuary.", :sources ["Numbers 18:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Derivation of arevut from the communal atonement language of the eglah arufah ritual.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "כפר לעמך ישראל אשר פדית", :spells "כליא", :letters ["כ" "ל" "י" "א" "פ"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :gematria, :a "כליא", :a-value 61, :b "זה הכהנים", :b-value 142, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timest